    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A popcorn ceiling, the lumpy sprayed-on texture known in the UK as Artex or a textured ceiling coating, can make an otherwise lovely rented flat feel dated. Homeowners can have it professionally removed or skimmed. Renters can’t, and shouldn’t try. You can still make it fade into the background, though, mostly by changing how the ceiling is lit and where the eye goes when you walk into the room.</p>
<p>This guide covers renter-friendly, deposit-safe ways to hide a popcorn ceiling, starting with the one thing you must never do.</p>

<h2 id="asbestos-caution">First: an important asbestos caution</h2>
<p>Older textured ceiling coatings, both American-style popcorn ceilings and Artex-type finishes in the UK, may contain asbestos. In the UK, coatings applied before asbestos was banned in 1999 are the ones to be most careful about. You can’t tell by looking at it whether a ceiling contains asbestos.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Never scrape, sand, drill, screw into or steam a textured ceiling</strong> unless it has been tested and shown to be safe.</li>
<li><strong>Don’t stick anything to it.</strong> Adhesive hooks, tiles and tapes can pull bits of texture away when they’re removed, which disturbs the surface and will probably cost you part of your deposit.</li>
<li><strong>Ask your landlord or letting agent</strong> whether the ceiling has been tested. If you want any work done, testing and removal are the landlord’s job and should be carried out by qualified professionals.</li>
<li>If the texture is already flaking or damaged, report it to your landlord in writing and leave it alone.</li>
</ul>
<p>Everything below works without touching the ceiling at all.</p>

<h2 id="why-texture-shows">Why the texture looks worse at certain times of day</h2>
<p>Texture shows up because of shadow. When light hits a bumpy surface at a shallow angle (known as grazing light), every peak casts a small shadow and the ceiling looks rough and busy. When light hits it straight on, or is soft and diffused, those shadows almost disappear.</p>
<p>That is why a popcorn ceiling can look fine at midday and terrible in the evening under a single flush ceiling light, or when an uplighter is placed close to the wall. Most of the work of hiding it is about getting rid of grazing light.</p>

<h2 id="lighting-placement">Light placement that flattens texture</h2>
<p>Lighting changes will make the biggest difference, and none of them need tools.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Avoid uplighters close to walls.</strong> Floor uplighters and wall-washing lamps pushed into corners send light skimming across the ceiling, which shows off every bump. If you love an uplighter, move it at least 60–90 cm (2–3 ft) away from the wall towards the middle of the room, so the light meets the ceiling more directly.</li>
<li><strong>Swap bare or open-topped shades for ones that direct light down.</strong> Table and floor lamps with opaque or lined drum shades put a pool of light at furniture level and leave the ceiling in soft shadow.</li>
<li><strong>Use several low lamps instead of one overhead light.</strong> Three or four lamps around a living room give even, warm light with no harsh shadows. Our <a href="/blog/layered-lighting-room-by-room-guide">layered lighting guide</a> explains how to balance them room by room.</li>
<li><strong>Choose warm, diffused bulbs.</strong> Frosted or opal bulbs around 2700K soften the light. Clear filament bulbs and bright cool-white bulbs make texture look sharper.</li>
<li><strong>Change the shade on the existing ceiling pendant.</strong> If there is a hanging pendant, a new shade that pushes most light downwards (a dome, a deep lined drum or an opaque metal shade) is a simple swap. Turn the power off at the switch and check the shade fits the lampholder and the bulb’s wattage limit. Replacing the fitting itself is electrical work, needs your landlord’s permission and should be done by a qualified electrician.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Check the room at night with each lamp on in turn. Whichever one makes the ceiling look rough is either too close to a wall or pointing too far upwards.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="plug-in-pendants">Statement pendants without ceiling fixings</h2>
<p>A dramatic light fitting gives the eye something to settle on so it stops looking at the ceiling around it. In a rental with a textured ceiling, it has to work without new fixings in that ceiling.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Arc floor lamps</strong> put a pendant-style shade over a sofa or dining table with no ceiling contact at all. Make sure the base is heavy and stable, and keep it out of walkways.</li>
<li><strong>Plug-in pendant (swag) kits</strong> plug into a wall socket and hang from a hook. In a textured-ceiling room, don’t screw a hook into the ceiling. Hang the light from a wall-mounted bracket or a sturdy freestanding lamp stand instead, use only hooks rated well above the light’s weight, and get written permission before making any holes in walls.</li>
<li><strong>Hang them low.</strong> A pendant dropped to around 75–90 cm (30–36 in) above a table brings the focus down to eye level. See our <a href="/blog/dining-room-lighting-chandelier-size-and-height">chandelier size and height guide</a> for proportions.</li>
<li><strong>Run cables neatly</strong> along skirting with removable cable clips, and never trap a cable under a rug where it can overheat or wear through.</li>
</ul>
<!--figure:living-room-shelves-paper-lanterns|Paper lantern pendants, open shelves and a cream sofa keep the eye at furniture height in a relaxed living room.-->

<h2 id="draw-the-eye-down">Draw the eye down with art, plants and furniture</h2>
<p>People notice whatever is at eye level first. If the walls and floor are interesting, the ceiling becomes background.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Go big with art at eye level.</strong> One large piece, or a tight gallery arrangement, centred around 145–150 cm (57–60 in) from the floor is better than lots of small frames scattered high up. In a rental, lean large frames on a sideboard or picture ledge, or use removable hanging strips rated for the weight, tested first on a hidden spot.</li>
<li><strong>Add tall plants, but not too tall.</strong> A fiddle-leaf fig or olive tree that stops around 30–45 cm (12–18 in) below the ceiling adds height and greenery without leading the eye right up to the texture.</li>
<li><strong>Use a patterned or textured rug.</strong> A strong rug anchors the room at floor level and gives the eye something to look at.</li>
<li><strong>Keep furniture low and horizontal.</strong> Low sofas, long sideboards and horizontal shelving lead the eye across the room, not up.</li>
<li><strong>Don’t angle mirrors upwards.</strong> A mirror tilted back reflects the ceiling and doubles the problem. Hang or lean mirrors upright, facing a window or a nice view.</li>
</ul>
<!--figure:living-room-abstract-art|A large abstract painting above a curved sofa gives the eye a strong focal point at seating height.-->

<h2 id="colour-tricks">Colour tricks that help</h2>
<p>You probably can’t paint the ceiling. Paint can loosen the texture, and on an untested coating it isn’t a renter’s decision to make. Colour elsewhere in the room can still help.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Warm, mid-tone walls</strong> (soft clay, mushroom, sage) look heavier and more interesting than the ceiling, so they hold attention. Get written permission before painting, and agree whether you’ll need to repaint before you move out.</li>
<li><strong>Avoid high-gloss finishes</strong> on nearby surfaces such as wall panels or furniture placed close to the ceiling. They bounce light up at a low angle and make texture more obvious.</li>
<li><strong>Colour through textiles</strong> works if painting isn’t allowed: curtains, cushions, throws and a rug in one warm palette pull the eye down. Our <a href="/blog/living-room-colour-scheme-guide">living room colour scheme guide</a> has palettes to start from.</li>
<li><strong>Hang curtains at a normal height.</strong> Curtains mounted right up at the ceiling lead the eye straight to it. A pole 10–15 cm (4–6 in) above the window frame keeps the eye lower, and it uses existing fixings if your rental already has them.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="tiles-and-fabric">Ceiling tiles and fabric draping: proceed with caution</h2>
<p>You’ll see lightweight “removable” ceiling tiles and fabric-draped ceilings suggested online. In a rental with a textured ceiling, think carefully before trying either.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Stick-on ceiling tiles</strong> are not suitable for popcorn or Artex ceilings. The adhesive can’t grip the bumpy surface properly, and taking the tiles off can pull texture away with them. Some polystyrene ceiling tiles also create a fire risk. Don’t use them without your landlord’s agreement and a tested ceiling.</li>
<li><strong>Fabric draping</strong> should never be attached to the ceiling itself. A freestanding canopy over a bed or a seating nook, or a fabric panel hung from an existing curtain pole, gives a similar softening effect.</li>
<li>Any fabric used overhead should be <strong>flame-retardant</strong> and kept well away from light fittings, bulbs, smoke alarms and heat sources. Never cover or obstruct a smoke or heat alarm, and check your tenancy agreement and any building fire rules, especially in flats.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="quick-checklist">A quick deposit-safe checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>Nothing screwed, stuck, painted or scraped onto the ceiling.</li>
<li>No uplighters close to walls. Use downward-facing, diffused lamps.</li>
<li>One strong focal light hung low from an arc lamp or a wall-hung plug-in pendant, never from a ceiling hook.</li>
<li>Large art, plants and a rug at eye and floor level.</li>
<li>Written landlord permission for any paint or wall fixings, with removable products tested on a hidden spot first.</li>
</ul>

